  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 239 AM MDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nHot weather is locked in for this weekend as a dominating ridge \nof high pressure remains centered across the Central Rockies. Mid \nlevel temperatures warm another 1C today and potentially another \n0.5-1C on Sunday. That means we'll be pushing to near 100F across \nall of the I-25 Corridor and plains this afternoon, and likely to \nor exceeding the century mark on Sunday when a few of the lower \nelevations on the plains could be very close to 105F. The Heat \nAdvisory remains in effect for the I-25 Urban Corridor for today, \nbut based on further warming and building heat impacts we have \nexpanded the Advisory to all of the northeast plains for Sunday. \nDenver's records of 99F today (set in 1963 and 1931) and 100F \ntomorrow (set in 1910) could both be tied or broken. \n\nRegarding convective potential, mid level water vapor imagery has\njuiced up rather quickly after yesterday's brief drying. In fact,\nthe latest models and ensembles boost our precipitable water (PW)\nto near 1.20-1.25\" for the Denver area by 00Z, or a good 150% of \nnormal. With such strong surface heating, we don't see much \nevidence of a cap in/near the I-25 Corridor and mountains. \nTherefore, we expect scattered coverage of storms along the I-25 \nCorridor, with numerous showers and thunderstorms over the Front \nRange mountains and Park County. The airmass is weakly capped over\nthe plains so we'll gradually taper off the chances of any rain \nto the east. With those relatively high PW values, warm cloud \ndepths up to 3,000 ft, and mean storm motion less than 15 mph, \nlocally heavy rain will be possible. That said, most areas will \nonly see gusty winds and light rain as typical in these scenarios.\nOn Sunday, there area signs of some slight drying although that \nmay be overdone given little if any moisture advection under the \nridge.\n\nOn Monday, the ridge center is forecast to shift slightly\nsouthward. That would allow a better push of monsoonal moisture to\nstream into the forecast area. It will still be hot, as latest\nmodels have backed off on any meaningful cooling. At least there \nwill be more heat relief due to late day outflow from showers and \nstorms. We think coverage will be boosted as better moisture \narrives, and with PW increasing further to 150-175% of normal \nlocally heavy rainfall can be expected with the stronger storms. \nSimilar if not even higher monsoonal moisture will hold across \nthe forecast area Tuesday and Wednesday per latest ensemble \nguidance. Thus, a couple more active weather days are expected \nalong with just slight cooling. Again, the main threats will be \nlocally heavy rainfall and localized flooding. At this time, we \nsee flood threats comparable to last Wednesday when spotty but \nvery intense rainfall occurred. Warm cloud depths have a chance to\ndeepen as temperatures cool slightly Tuesday into Wednesday, so \nsomething to watch over the next few days. \n\nFor the latter portions of the week through next weekend, the \nupper level ridge is forecast to retrograde into the Great Basin.\nThis puts more of a northwest flow aloft across Colorado, which\ncould lead to some mid/upper level drying. However, at the same\ntime we have opportunity for a backdoor weak \"cool\" front or two\nwhich could aid low level moisture and instability over the\nplains. Temperatures will still average a few degrees above normal\nduring this period, while chances of storms gradually decrease\ntoward next weekend.",
